The "hello world" of concurrency. The way you implement it immediately reveals your level of fluency.

**`ReentrantLock` + two `Condition` instances** (this is the version to present):

<pre><code><span class="k">class</span> BoundedQueue&lt;T&gt; {
    <span class="k">private final</span> Queue&lt;T&gt; q = <span class="k">new</span> ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();
    <span class="k">private final</span> <span class="k">int</span> cap;
    <span class="k">private final</span> ReentrantLock lock = <span class="k">new</span> ReentrantLock();
    <span class="k">private final</span> Condition notFull  = lock.<span class="f">newCondition</span>();
    <span class="k">private final</span> Condition notEmpty = lock.<span class="f">newCondition</span>();
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="f">BoundedQueue</span>(<span class="k">int</span> cap) { <span class="k">this</span>.cap = cap; }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">put</span>(T x) <span class="k">throws</span> InterruptedException {
        lock.<span class="f">lockInterruptibly</span>();
        <span class="k">try</span> {
            <span class="k">while</span> (q.<span class="f">size</span>() == cap) notFull.<span class="f">await</span>();   <span class="c">// WHILE, not IF</span>
            q.<span class="f">add</span>(x);
            notEmpty.<span class="f">signal</span>();                        <span class="c">// wake only a consumer</span>
        } <span class="k">finally</span> { lock.<span class="f">unlock</span>(); }
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public</span> T <span class="f">take</span>() <span class="k">throws</span> InterruptedException {
        lock.<span class="f">lockInterruptibly</span>();
        <span class="k">try</span> {
            <span class="k">while</span> (q.<span class="f">isEmpty</span>()) notEmpty.<span class="f">await</span>();
            T x = q.<span class="f">poll</span>();
            notFull.<span class="f">signal</span>();
            <span class="k">return</span> x;
        } <span class="k">finally</span> { lock.<span class="f">unlock</span>(); }
    }
}</code></pre>

**The `wait`/`notify` version** — the same logic, but there is only [[o:one]] wait set, so `notifyAll()` is mandatory:

<pre><code><span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">synchronized</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">put</span>(T x) <span class="k">throws</span> InterruptedException {
    <span class="k">while</span> (q.<span class="f">size</span>() == cap) <span class="f">wait</span>();
    q.<span class="f">add</span>(x);
    <span class="f">notifyAll</span>();          <span class="c">// notify() may wake another producer =&gt; possible liveness failure</span>
}</code></pre>

:::warn Three classic mistakes
[[r:`if` instead of `while`]] — spurious wakeups and contention after a wakeup can make the condition false again, so it must be rechecked.
[[r:`notify()` with one shared wait set]] — it can wake a thread of the wrong type, one that is waiting for the wrong condition, and the entire system can hang.
[[r:`unlock()` outside `finally`]] — an exception partway through means the lock is never released.
:::

:::deep
- The decisive advantage of `Condition` is [[g:two separate wait sets]] → `signal()` (wake one) instead of `notifyAll()` (wake everyone and let them contend). On a heavily used queue, the performance difference is substantial.
- `await()` [[o:releases the lock]] while waiting and reacquires it before returning. Without understanding that, it is impossible to explain why both sides can wait under the same lock without deadlocking.
- In production, use the built-in `ArrayBlockingQueue`/`LinkedBlockingQueue`. [[o:Mention that]], then still implement it by hand, because that is what the interviewer wants to see.
- `ArrayBlockingQueue` uses one lock for both ends; `LinkedBlockingQueue` uses [[g:two locks]] (separate put/take locks), so it can deliver higher throughput with multiple producers and consumers.
- Common follow-ups: `offer(x, timeout)`, where `notFull.await(t, unit)` returns `false` on timeout; and graceful queue shutdown using a `closed` flag plus `signalAll`.
- For a completely lock-free queue, use `ConcurrentLinkedQueue`, but it is [[r:neither blocking nor bounded]], so it does not solve this problem.
:::

LC 1114/1115. The problem: two (or three) threads must produce output in the required order regardless of scheduling order.

**Approach 1 — `Semaphore`** (the most concise and the best one to present):

<pre><code><span class="k">class</span> FooBar {
    <span class="k">private final</span> <span class="k">int</span> n;
    <span class="k">private final</span> Semaphore fooReady = <span class="k">new</span> Semaphore(<span class="n">1</span>);   <span class="c">// foo goes first</span>
    <span class="k">private final</span> Semaphore barReady = <span class="k">new</span> Semaphore(<span class="n">0</span>);
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">foo</span>(Runnable printFoo) <span class="k">throws</span> InterruptedException {
        <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> i = <span class="n">0</span>; i &lt; n; i++) {
            fooReady.<span class="f">acquire</span>();
            printFoo.<span class="f">run</span>();
            barReady.<span class="f">release</span>();          <span class="c">// hand the turn to bar</span>
        }
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">bar</span>(Runnable printBar) <span class="k">throws</span> InterruptedException {
        <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">int</span> i = <span class="n">0</span>; i &lt; n; i++) {
            barReady.<span class="f">acquire</span>();
            printBar.<span class="f">run</span>();
            fooReady.<span class="f">release</span>();
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

The idea: [[g:each thread has its own permit]]. After printing, it hands the permit to the next thread. The initial permit counts determine who goes first.

**Approach 2 — `synchronized` + `wait/notifyAll` + a turn variable:**

<pre><code><span class="k">private</span> <span class="k">int</span> turn = <span class="n">0</span>;
<span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">synchronized</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">foo</span>(Runnable p) <span class="k">throws</span> InterruptedException {
    <span class="k">while</span> (turn != <span class="n">0</span>) <span class="f">wait</span>();
    p.<span class="f">run</span>(); turn = <span class="n">1</span>; <span class="f">notifyAll</span>();
}</code></pre>

This approach scales easily to three or more threads (`turn` cycles through them), but `notifyAll` wakes threads unnecessarily.

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Drawbacks</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Semaphore</td><td>concise, no explicit lock, clear intent</td><td>more threads require more semaphores</td></tr>
<tr><td>wait/notifyAll + turn</td><td>scales easily to n threads</td><td>unnecessary wakeups; must remember <code>while</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Lock + n Condition</td><td>precise wakeups</td><td>the most verbose</td></tr>
<tr><td>CyclicBarrier</td><td>suited to round-based synchronization</td><td>does not impose order within a round by itself</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

:::deep
- [[r:Do not use `volatile` plus busy-waiting]] (`while (turn != 0) {}`). It produces the right output but burns CPU. The interviewer will immediately ask, *"What if the other thread is slow?"*
- `Semaphore` generalizes to `n` threads: use a `Semaphore[n]` array; thread `i` waits on `sem[i]` and releases `sem[(i+1)%n]`. Explaining the general solution earns points.
- The `printNumber` variant (LC 1116, zero-even-odd) follows the same pattern: one semaphore each for `zero`, `even`, and `odd`.
- `Semaphore(1)` differs from `ReentrantLock`: a semaphore [[o:has no owner]], so thread A may acquire it and thread B may release it. That is precisely why it fits this problem while a lock does not.
- `Exchanger` is the right tool when two threads need to exchange paired *data*, not merely hand off turns.
- Always cover the edge cases: when `n` is zero, nothing is printed; and every thread must be able to exit, with none left blocked in `acquire` after the final round.
:::

Five philosophers, five forks, and each philosopher needs the two adjacent forks. If everyone picks up the left fork first, the result is a perfect [[r:deadlock]].

This problem is really about the four Coffman conditions and four ways to break them.

**Approach 1 — Resource ordering** (the most common approach; breaks "circular wait"):

<pre><code><span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">wantsToEat</span>(<span class="k">int</span> p, Runnable pickLeft, Runnable pickRight,
                        Runnable eat, Runnable putLeft, Runnable putRight) {
    <span class="k">int</span> left = p, right = (p + <span class="n">1</span>) % <span class="n">5</span>;
    <span class="k">int</span> first = Math.<span class="f">min</span>(left, right), second = Math.<span class="f">max</span>(left, right);
<span class="c">    </span>
    forks[first].<span class="f">lock</span>();                <span class="c">// ALWAYS acquire the lower-numbered fork first</span>
    forks[second].<span class="f">lock</span>();
    <span class="k">try</span> {
        pickLeft.<span class="f">run</span>(); pickRight.<span class="f">run</span>();
        eat.<span class="f">run</span>();
        putLeft.<span class="f">run</span>(); putRight.<span class="f">run</span>();
    } <span class="k">finally</span> {
        forks[second].<span class="f">unlock</span>(); forks[first].<span class="f">unlock</span>();
    }
}</code></pre>

**Approach 2 — Limit how many philosophers may sit at the table** (breaks "hold and wait"): use `Semaphore(4)` for five philosophers, ensuring that at least one can always acquire both forks.

**Approach 3 — Asymmetry**: even-numbered philosophers pick up the left fork first; odd-numbered philosophers pick up the right one first.

**Approach 4 — `tryLock` with a timeout** (breaks "no preemption"): if a philosopher cannot acquire both forks, [[r:release everything]] and retry. Watch for [[o:livelock]]; randomized backoff is required.

:::deep
- The four Coffman conditions (all four are necessary for deadlock): [[b:mutual exclusion]], [[b:hold and wait]], [[b:no preemption]], and [[b:circular wait]]. Each approach above breaks one condition. Framing the answer this way makes the reasoning rigorous.
- [[g:Resource ordering]] is the most broadly applicable technique in production: when transferring money between two accounts, always lock them in ascending ID order. Connecting this to 5.10 (database deadlocks) is a strong bonus.
- `tryLock` without backoff can produce livelock: everyone repeatedly acquires and releases in lockstep, so nobody gets to eat. The CPUs remain busy, yet the system makes no progress; recognizing that distinction demonstrates depth.
- Fairness: `new ReentrantLock(true)` prevents starvation but is measurably slower. State the trade-off instead of choosing one policy by default.
- A fifth concern that is often overlooked is [[o:starvation]]. It is not deadlock: the system continues to run, but one philosopher never gets a turn. Approach 2 and fair locks address it.
- Production mapping: forks can represent database row locks, pooled connections, or file locks. It is the same problem with different names.
:::

[[b:CAS]] (compare-and-swap) is an atomic CPU instruction: *"If the memory location still equals `expected`, replace it with `newValue`."* The entire `java.util.concurrent.atomic` package is built on it.

<pre><code><span class="c">// The essence of incrementAndGet: a retry loop</span>
<span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">int</span> <span class="f">increment</span>() {
    <span class="k">int</span> cur, next;
    <span class="k">do</span> {
        cur  = value.<span class="f">get</span>();
        next = cur + <span class="n">1</span>;
    } <span class="k">while</span> (!value.<span class="f">compareAndSet</span>(cur, next));   <span class="c">// on failure, reread and retry</span>
    <span class="k">return</span> next;
}</code></pre>

No locking and no blocking: a losing thread simply retries. This is [[g:lock-free]] — at least one thread can always make progress.

**Lock-free stack (Treiber stack):**

<pre><code><span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">push</span>(T item) {
    Node&lt;T&gt; newHead = <span class="k">new</span> Node&lt;&gt;(item);
    Node&lt;T&gt; oldHead;
    <span class="k">do</span> {
        oldHead = head.<span class="f">get</span>();
        newHead.next = oldHead;
    } <span class="k">while</span> (!head.<span class="f">compareAndSet</span>(oldHead, newHead));
}</code></pre>

**The ABA problem:** thread 1 reads `head = A`. Before it performs CAS, thread 2 pops A, pops B, and then pushes A back. Thread 1's CAS [[r:still succeeds]] because the head is again `A`, but `A.next` has changed and the stack is corrupted.

The solution is to [[g:attach a version number]]. `AtomicStampedReference` compares both the reference and the stamp.

<pre><code>AtomicStampedReference&lt;Node&gt; head = <span class="k">new</span> AtomicStampedReference&lt;&gt;(<span class="k">null</span>, <span class="n">0</span>);
head.<span class="f">compareAndSet</span>(oldHead, newHead, stamp, stamp + <span class="n">1</span>);</code></pre>

:::deep
- CAS excels under [[o:low contention]]. Under high contention, retry loops burn CPU and can be [[r:slower than locks]]; a lock can at least park the thread.
- Use [[g:`LongAdder` instead of `AtomicLong`]] for a write-heavy counter. It spreads updates across multiple cells and combines them on read, delivering far higher throughput under contention. The trade-off is that `sum()` is only a weakly consistent snapshot. This is a very concrete point to mention.
- Because Java uses GC for memory management, ABA is [[o:less dangerous]] than in C++: an address is not reclaimed and reassigned to an unrelated object. It can still occur when nodes are deliberately reused.
- `AtomicReference.updateAndGet(fn)` packages the CAS loop for you. The [[r:function must be pure]] and free of side effects because it may be invoked multiple times.
- Distinguish the progress guarantees: [[o:lock-free]] (some thread always makes progress) is stronger than [[o:obstruction-free]] and weaker than [[o:wait-free]] (every thread completes within a finite number of steps).
- `volatile` provides visibility only; CAS provides both visibility and atomicity. This is one of the most common conceptual follow-ups (see 1.1, JMM).
- Java 9+ provides `VarHandle` instead of `Unsafe` for low-level atomic operations; knowing the name is sufficient.
:::

A virtual thread is a Java `Thread` scheduled by the [[o:JVM]] over carrier threads. It is cheap enough to create per task; supported blocking operations park and unmount it so the carrier can run other work.

<pre><code><span class="c">// One virtual thread per task; this executor is not a reusable thread pool</span>
<span class="k">try</span> (<span class="k">var</span> executor = Executors.<span class="f">newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor</span>()) {
    <span class="k">for</span> (<span class="k">var</span> id : bookingIds) {
        executor.<span class="f">submit</span>(() -&gt; {
            <span class="k">var</span> price = priceApi.<span class="f">get</span>(id);
            <span class="k">var</span> seats = seatApi.<span class="f">get</span>(id);
            <span class="k">return new</span> Page(price, seats);
        });
    }
} <span class="c">// close() waits for submitted tasks</span></code></pre>

The main benefit is [[g:structured-looking blocking code]] with ordinary stack traces, exceptions, and debugger behavior at high I/O concurrency. It is not automatically faster than reactive code, and it does not add backpressure.

**JDK 25 preview API — manage related task lifetimes together:**

<pre><code><span class="c">// Compile and run with --enable-preview on JDK 25</span>
<span class="k">try</span> (<span class="k">var</span> scope = StructuredTaskScope.<span class="f">open</span>()) {
    <span class="k">var</span> price = scope.<span class="f">fork</span>(() -&gt; priceApi.<span class="f">get</span>(id));
    <span class="k">var</span> seats = scope.<span class="f">fork</span>(() -&gt; seatApi.<span class="f">get</span>(id));
    scope.<span class="f">join</span>();                         <span class="c">// failure cancels the sibling and propagates</span>
    <span class="k">return new</span> Page(price.<span class="f">get</span>(), seats.<span class="f">get</span>());
}</code></pre>

:::warn Pinning guidance changed in JDK 24
On JDK 21–23, blocking while holding a `synchronized` monitor could pin the carrier, so moving I/O out of the monitor or using `ReentrantLock` was a valid targeted fix. JEP 491 removed monitor-related pinning in JDK 24. Native/foreign calls and class-initialization edge cases can still hold a carrier, but replacing every monitor is no longer sound advice.
:::

:::deep
- [[r:Do not pool virtual threads]]. Create one per task; use a semaphore or the downstream's real pool to bound scarce capacity.
- `ThreadLocal` remains compatible, but per-thread mutable state can be costly at large concurrency. `ScopedValue` is final in JDK 25 for immutable bounded context; it is not a replacement for all mutable thread-local state.
- CPU-bound work remains limited by cores. Use bounded parallelism and measure context-switch/queue overhead.
- A 20-connection database pool still admits only 20 concurrent queries. Cheap waiters can improve utilization, but excessive waiters consume memory and exhaust deadlines.
- For JDK 21–23, `jdk.VirtualThreadPinned` JFR data and `-Djdk.tracePinnedThreads` can locate monitor pinning. The system property was removed in JDK 24; on newer JDKs use JFR, JSON thread dumps, carrier utilization, and latency evidence.
- `StructuredTaskScope` is preview in JDK 25 and its constructors changed to `open(...)`; do not publish the older `new ShutdownOnFailure()` form as JDK 25 code.

Use reactive/event-loop I/O where explicit streaming and backpressure fit the whole stack; use virtual threads where blocking APIs and sequential control flow improve maintainability. Compare the broader models in (15-network-i-o-models.realtime-event-driven.q3).
:::
